    "result_text": "The change is complete and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented a rule that rejects creating an owner whose normalized telephone is already used by another owner, responding with **409 Conflict**.\n\nChanges made (following standard Spring layering):\n\n1. **`DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending telephone, mirroring the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`/`MissingOwnerFieldsException` pattern.\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` that maps `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException` to `HttpStatus.CONFLICT` (409) with an `errors: [\"telephone\"]` ProblemDetail, consistent with the other owner-field handlers.\n\n3. **`OwnerRepository`** + all three implementations (`SpringDataOwnerRepository` via `@Query`, `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl` via JPQL, `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` via SQL) \u2014 added `findByTelephone(String)`. All three are updated so the code compiles under any active profile, even though the tests run under `spring-data-jpa`.\n\n4. **`ClinicService`** / **`ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 added `findOwnerByTelephone(String)` as a read-only transactional facade method.\n\n5. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 after normalizing the telephone (reusing the existing `normalizeTelephone`, which strips to exactly 10 digits), it checks whether any owner already has that normalized value and throws `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException` if so, before saving.\n\nBecause the telephone is normalized *before* the uniqueness check and before persistence, two payloads that normalize to the same digits collide correctly. The check runs in the same transaction as the save, so JPA auto-flush makes prior creates visible.\n\n**Tests:** `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `coreRejectsDuplicateTelephone` (expects 409) and `functionalityAllowsDistinctTelephone` (expects 2xx) both pass; full suite is `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
